[38;5;231m⏺[39m Here's the design.

  [1mApproach.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m gains a trailing third parameter, [38;5;153muserId[39m, and includes it in the line it already logs. The submit
  handler reads the value from a new hidden input and passes it through. Trailing keeps the existing [38;5;153m(username, [39m
  [38;5;153mpassword)[39m order intact, so the one existing call site is the only thing that changes.

  [1mFiles touched — two:[0m

  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — add a hidden input inside the form, next to the existing fields:
  [90m<[34minput[90m [36mtype[90m=[31m"hidden"[90m [36mid[90m=[31m"user-id"[90m />[39m
  It ships with no value; whatever populates it (server-rendered markup, or a manual value while you test) is outside
  this change.

  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — three small edits:
  - [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m — signature gains the trailing param.
  - Its [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m becomes [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging in:", username, "userId:", userId)[39m.
  - The submit handler reads [38;5;153mdocument.getElementById("user-id").value[39m and passes it as the third argument.

  [1mWhat I'm deliberately not touching.[0m [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m keeps checking only username and password — the userId isn't
  user-entered, so failing the form on a missing hidden field would block a real login over a markup problem. The return
  value stays [38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m as you chose. And [38;5;153mlogin[39m remains a stub that never posts to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m; this change
  doesn't make the tracking reach a server.

  [1mTesting.[0m There's no test infrastructure here — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts or dev dependencies, and no test files
  exist. So I won't be adding tests to a nonexistent harness as part of this. Verification is manual: open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m,
  set the hidden input's value in devtools, submit, and confirm the console line carries the userId. Say the word if
  you'd rather I stand up a test runner first — that's a larger change and I'd scope it separately.

  [1mOne thing to be aware of.[0m With the hidden field empty by default, the log will read [38;5;153muserId: [39m (empty string) on every
  real submit until something populates it. The wiring is what makes this feature actually track anyone; this change
  builds the path it travels.

  Does this look right? I'll implement once you confirm.

